  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and checked the dashboard and the alert rule by reading them back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' covers the `kibana_sample_data_flights` index. Its time field is `timestamp`. It also has the `Carrier` field (text), the `Cancelled` field (true/false), and fields for origin, destination, delay, distance and price.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-012190c9 Flights' (id `s4-012190c9-flights-849df332a50a`) with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart counting flights over `timestamp`.\n   - The carrier panel: a bar chart counting flights for the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-012190c9 Cancelled flights' (id `ba131afe-c57f-45ec-b3b3-5676822146da`). It searches the flights index for flights where `Cancelled` is true, checks every minute, and fires when there are more than 10 in a 5-minute window. It was created already enabled, so no separate enable step was needed. The rule has no notification actions attached, so it records alerts but won't send any messages.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the carrier panel to 'Flights by carrier', and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
